# Android release signing — copy to key.properties and fill in.
#
# key.properties, *.jks and *.keystore are all gitignored. This example is the
# only one of the four that belongs in the repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Create the upload keystore, ONCE, somewhere outside this repository.
#
#    PowerShell (^ is cmd's line continuation; in PowerShell use ` or one line):
#
#      keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore C:\Users\<you>\keys\nph-upload.jks `
#        -storetype JKS -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 -alias upload
#
#    -storetype JKS is explicit because modern JDKs default to PKCS12, which
#    works but emits a conversion warning on every build.
#
#    -validity 10000 is ~27 years. Google requires a key valid past 22 October
#    2033 and recommends at least 25 years; a key that expires means you can
#    never ship another update under the same listing.
#
# 2. Back the keystore up somewhere durable and private — a password manager
#    with file attachments, or an encrypted archive. This is the UPLOAD key, so
#    losing it is recoverable: Play support can reset it, provided Play App
#    Signing is enabled. The APP SIGNING key Google holds cannot be replaced at
#    all, which is the whole reason Play App Signing exists.
#
# 3. Enable Play App Signing when you create the Play Console listing. You
#    upload bundles signed with the key above; Google re-signs them for
#    distribution with a key it keeps.
#
# 4. Build the bundle Play wants — an AAB, not an APK:
#
#      flutter build appbundle --release
#
#    The APK is for sideloading onto a test device.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

storePassword=
keyPassword=
keyAlias=upload

# Absolute path, forward slashes even on Windows. Resolved relative to
# apps/mobile/android/ if relative, so an absolute path outside the repository
# is the safer choice.
storeFile=C:/Users/<you>/keys/nph-upload.jks
